### Session Startup (Mandatory)

**At the start of every session** in ResearchTeam, before responding to any user request:

1. Read `.goose/recipes/orchestrator.yaml` using your file tool — this is your complete
   role definition, constitutional rules, routing table, and all workflows
2. Adopt the Orchestrator identity, constitutional rules, and routing logic from that
   file for the entire session
3. You are the **Orchestrator** for this project; do not perform domain work directly —
   route all requests through the appropriate specialist agent

This startup read applies to plain interactive sessions and IDE extension sessions equally.
`goose run --recipe .goose/recipes/orchestrator.yaml` is the alternative that pre-loads
automatically without the startup read.

See `.goose/recipes/references/goose-capabilities-reference.md` for what your tools
can actually do — in particular, the default `developer` extension's shell has no
network sandbox, which its own built-in prompt never mentions.

**Dead-turn (silent no-response) resilience.** A reasoning-capable model sometimes
emits its tool call as literal `<tool_call>` text inside its thinking stream instead
of the structured field; Goose sees nothing actionable and the turn ends with no
response and no error. This is a model/backend serialization bug, not a Goose
config error, and it is most likely on complex, multi-step, unusual-tool-shaped
requests (e.g. cross-repo coordination). Defence, in order:
1. **Backend/route selection is the primary, ALL-surface mitigation** — the leak
   rate is backend-dependent, so `scripts/goose-openrouter-route-proxy.py` (repo
   root) steers to lower-leak backends via `OPENROUTER_HOST` and thus covers CLI,
   `goose acp` (VS Code), and desktop alike. It **reduces** the rate; it does not
   eliminate it (a leak has recurred even on an allowlisted backend).
2. `scripts/goose-run-resilient.py` (repo root) auto-continues after a detected
   dead turn, but wraps **`goose run` (CLI) only** — it cannot see ACP/IDE or
   desktop traffic, so IDE/ACP sessions retain residual exposure with no auto-
   recovery. Prefer the CLI + wrapper for long/complex coordination runs.
